      "reveal": "The suffix <span lang=\"de\">-ung</span> turns verbs into abstract nouns, cognate with English \"-ing\" (and guess what? It's also 100% feminine!). Notice how German drops the verb's <span lang=\"de\">-en</span> ending when <span lang=\"de\">-ung</span> attaches.\n\n<span lang=\"de\">Weg</span> ~ \"way\". To <span lang=\"de\">beweg(en)</span> is to \"be on your way\", i.e. \"to move\". An even more surprising cognate is \"weigh\", with the meaning possibly evolving from \"move\" → \"lift\" → \"assess weight by lifting\". The original meaning is preserved in the phrase \"Anchors aweigh!\" ⚓"
